WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:03.300
Most stablecoin hacks don't break any code.

00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:06.130
They use the issuer's own keys.

00:00:06.704 --> 00:00:10.764
In March, Resolv lost about twenty-five million dollars this way.

00:00:10.934 --> 00:00:13.856
It took about three hours to stop.

00:00:13.886 --> 00:00:17.794
Levee is a circuit breaker for stablecoin issuers on Hedera.

00:00:17.824 --> 00:00:21.224
It has one rule: no receipt, no movement.

00:00:22.584 --> 00:00:24.964
Think of the issuer's treasury as a vault.

00:00:24.994 --> 00:00:28.478
Before any coins leave, Levee writes a receipt on Hedera.

00:00:28.508 --> 00:00:30.714
Only then do the coins go out.

00:00:30.744 --> 00:00:34.144
A watcher checks every move against its receipt.

00:00:36.427 --> 00:00:40.578
Now a thief steals the keys and grabs coins, with no receipt.

00:00:40.608 --> 00:00:42.725
The watcher spots it in seconds.

00:00:42.755 --> 00:00:46.155
The vault locks, and the coins come back.

00:00:48.647 --> 00:00:52.642
To open the vault again, two of three admins must turn their keys.

00:00:52.672 --> 00:00:56.972
Levee can close the vault, but it can never open it.

00:00:58.196 --> 00:01:01.548
Here's the real thing, running live on Hedera testnet.

00:01:01.578 --> 00:01:04.322
Across the top: the treasury, total supply,

00:01:04.352 --> 00:01:07.182
payments matched, and zero false alarms.

00:01:07.352 --> 00:01:09.463
Every payment walks four steps.

00:01:09.493 --> 00:01:11.737
Request, receipt, transfer, check.

00:01:11.907 --> 00:01:15.796
On the right, proof you can scan, and every move as it happens.

00:01:15.826 --> 00:01:17.625
This chip says the payment machine

00:01:17.655 --> 00:01:20.755
passed its health check, stored on Arkiv.

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:24.979
Let's send five hundred tokens to Asha Pay, a distributor.

00:01:25.009 --> 00:01:28.295
Levee checks the request, and writes the receipt first.

00:01:28.325 --> 00:01:32.629
Then a separate issuer signer finds it and adds the second signature.

00:01:32.659 --> 00:01:36.359
The transfer lands, and the check turns green: matched.

00:01:36.971 --> 00:01:39.378
Every step is a real transaction.

00:01:39.408 --> 00:01:41.908
Here's that transfer on HashScan.

00:01:42.384 --> 00:01:45.213
Now the hack: someone with the treasury keys sends

00:01:45.243 --> 00:01:48.491
ten thousand tokens straight out, with no receipt.

00:01:48.521 --> 00:01:51.718
The clock runs from the moment the theft hit the chain.

00:01:51.748 --> 00:01:53.881
Levee caught it at seven seconds.

00:01:53.911 --> 00:01:58.811
The treasury froze, and the tokens were taken back at ten and a half.

00:01:58.841 --> 00:02:00.946
Resolv took about three hours.

00:02:00.976 --> 00:02:04.976
On this run, Levee took ten and a half seconds.

00:02:05.209 --> 00:02:07.857
To reopen, two of three admins sign.

00:02:07.887 --> 00:02:10.687
And the treasury is open again.

00:02:10.726 --> 00:02:14.950
One more: a stolen minting key prints five thousand fake tokens.

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:16.485
No receipt again.

00:02:16.515 --> 00:02:18.743
Levee wipes them in about four seconds,

00:02:18.773 --> 00:02:22.144
and total supply is back where it was.

00:02:22.174 --> 00:02:24.084
Levee is built on Hedera.

00:02:24.114 --> 00:02:27.625
Stablecoin Studio, the Consensus Service for receipts,

00:02:27.655 --> 00:02:29.133
Scheduled Transactions,

00:02:29.163 --> 00:02:32.805
threshold keys, a verified contract and the Mirror Node,

00:02:32.835 --> 00:02:35.061
with health passes on Arkiv.

00:02:35.091 --> 00:02:39.228
It's open source, and live at levee-theta.vercel.app.

00:02:41.178 --> 00:02:43.678
Levee: no receipt, no movement.
